    "url": "/industries/banking/",
    "meta": {
      "title": "Banking Software Development Company | Clickmasters",
      "description": "Banking software development — digital banking experiences, core-adjacent platforms, lending workflows & integrations built examiner-ready for banks and credit unions."
    },
    "content": "---\n\n> **PAGE:** `/industries/banking/`  \n> **SOURCE FILE:** `wave0-batch6/ind06-banking.md`\n\n\n# INDUSTRY 06 — Banking\n\n\n\n# Banking Software Development Company\n\n**Banking software development for banks and credit unions is mostly *core-adjacent* development: building the digital experiences, lending workflows, and operational tools that live around the core banking system — because the core itself is where innovation goes to file a change request.** Clickmasters builds for US community banks, credit unions, and bank-tech companies with the posture this industry actually requires: examiner-ready documentation, vendor-diligence fluency, and architecture that treats the core as a system of record to integrate with, not a platform to fight.\n\nHere's the strategic frame we bring to every banking engagement: **your core (FIS, Fiserv, Jack Henry, or a challenger) is your ledger of record and your slowest-moving asset — and that's fine.** The competitive game is played in the layer above it: how fast you onboard a business account, how your lending team moves a file, what your digital experience feels like against the megabank apps your customers also carry. That layer is buildable on your timeline, not your core vendor's roadmap — through the APIs and integration channels the cores now (grudgingly, unevenly) expose. This page is about building that layer well. [Book a consultation](#consult) with a team that's been through vendor management review before.\n\n[Trust bar: FI clients · examinations passed with our systems in scope (if verifiable) · SOC 2 status]\n\n## What We Build for Financial Institutions\n\n| System | The job | Banking-specific engineering |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Digital account opening & onboarding** | Deposit and business accounts opened in minutes, not branch visits | KYC/KYB integration, CIP documentation trails, decisioning workflows with override audit |\n| **[Lending platforms](/solutions/loan-management/)** | Origination through servicing — consumer, commercial, SBA-adjacent | Document collection portals, underwriting queues, covenant tracking, examiner-ready decision trails |\n| **Business & treasury [portals](/solutions/customer-portal/)** | The RM-relationship experience: entitlements, approvals, reporting | Multi-user entitlement models, dual-control on money movement, positive-pay workflows |\n| **Core integration layers** | One [API surface](/services/api-development/) over the core for everything you build next | Core-vendor channel fluency, message-queue buffering, reconciliation reports against core truth |\n| **Back-office [workflow automation](/solutions/workflow-automation/)** | Exceptions, disputes, wire ops, BSA case queues | Every action logged, four-eyes controls, SLA dashboards for ops leadership |\n| **[Fraud & BSA/AML tooling](/solutions/fraud-detection/)** | Alert triage, case management, SAR-prep workflows | Analyst-first UX, disposition trails, model-governance documentation |\n| **[Banking AI](/services/ai-application-development/)** | The examiner-survivable use cases | Document intelligence on loan files, policy RAG for staff, agent-assisted alert triage — [human-accountable](/resources/ai-development/human-in-the-loop-ai/), model-risk-documented |\n\n## Built for the Examination, Not Just the Demo\n\nBank software is judged twice: by users, and by examiners. Our deliverables are designed for both audiences from day one: architecture and data-flow documentation in vendor-management-ready form · access controls with periodic-review evidence · immutable audit logs on every material action · change-management trails (what changed, who approved, when) · [tested DR with documented RTO/RPO](/resources/cloud-devops/disaster-recovery-rto-rpo/) · and SOC 2-aligned practices on our side of the relationship [link real status], because we know we're an entry in *your* third-party risk register — and we come prepared to be a boring one. Your compliance team gets a documentation package, not a discovery project.\n\n## The Ledger and Money-Movement Discipline\n\nEverything we said on the [FinTech page](/industries/fintech/) about ledgers applies doubly inside an FI: anything we build that touches balances reconciles to the core daily with break reports, money movement is [idempotent](/resources/software-engineering/idempotency-in-apis/) and dual-controlled, and corrections are reversing entries with reasons — never edits. [Ledger architecture →](/resources/architecture/fintech-ledger-reference-architecture/)\n\n## Recent Banking Work\n\n> **[PLACEHOLDER — 2–3 FI case studies: institution size, system delivered, examiner/audit outcome or ops metric (e.g., \"commercial onboarding from 9 days to same-day\") — conservative and verifiable]**\n\n## Cost Context\n\nPortals and workflow systems: **$80K–$250K**. Lending platforms and onboarding systems: **$150K–$500K+** (integration surface and compliance documentation are the drivers). Core-integration layers: **$60K–$180K** — and they're the multiplier investment: everything built afterward rides them. [Cost guides →](/resources/software-cost-guides/)\n\n## FAQ\n\n**Can you integrate with our core (FIS/Fiserv/Jack Henry)?**\nYes — through the vendors' API channels and integration programs, with the realism that access, sandboxes, and certifications run on the core vendor's clock. We scope that timeline explicitly and build against mocks in parallel so your project doesn't idle in their queue.\n\n**How do you handle our vendor due-diligence process?**\nBy arriving with the package: SOC 2 documentation [state real status], security posture, insurance certificates, financials as required, and reference FIs. We've been through the process; the goal is making your TPRM review short and your renewal automatic.\n\n**Can a community bank really compete digitally with the megabanks?**\nOn breadth, no; on the experiences that decide local relationships — business onboarding, lending speed, RM-service tooling — absolutely, because those are workflow problems, not budget problems. That's precisely the core-adjacent layer this page describes.\n\n**Is AI safe to deploy inside a bank?**\nDeployed with model-risk discipline, yes: documented use cases, human accountability on decisions, measured accuracy, and audit trails — the [SR 11-7-aware version](/resources/ai-development/enterprise-ai-governance/), not the demo version. Loan-file document intelligence and policy RAG for staff are where FIs are getting real value with manageable risk today.\n\n**Cloud or on-prem?**\nIncreasingly cloud, with examiner-familiar patterns: encryption, network isolation, logging, and [DR evidence](/resources/cloud-devops/hybrid-cloud-regulated-industries/). We build to your risk appetite and your examiner relationship — both are real inputs.\n\n**How long do banking builds take?**\nWorkflow tools and portals: 4–7 months. Lending and onboarding platforms: 7–14 months with core-integration certification in parallel. Realistic sequencing beats optimistic Gantt charts in this industry — we quote the former.\n\n---\n\n### [#consult] Book a Banking Technology Consultation\nBring your core, your pain points, and your examiner calendar — leave with a core-adjacent roadmap and an honest integration timeline.\n[Form] [Calendar embed]",
