    "content": "---\n\n> **PAGE:** `/industries/manufacturing/`  \n> **SOURCE FILE:** `wave0-batch5/ind05-manufacturing.md`\n\n\n# INDUSTRY 05 — Manufacturing\n\n\n\n# Manufacturing Software Development Company\n\n**Manufacturing software development is building the systems between the ERP and the machines: production tracking and MES-class platforms, quality management, shop-floor apps, scheduling tools, and the integration layer that makes the office's numbers match the floor's reality.** Clickmasters builds manufacturing software for US plants and industrial companies — engineered for environments where downtime is measured in dollars per minute and the users wear safety glasses, not headsets.\n\nEvery plant we walk into has the same architecture: an ERP that thinks it knows what's happening, machines that actually know, and between them — clipboards, whiteboards, and a heroic spreadsheet named something like `SCHEDULE_FINAL_v7_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx`. That gap is where scrap hides, where expedites are born, and where your data goes to become fiction. Manufacturing software's job is closing it: capturing reality at the point of work and reconciling it upward. This page is about doing that without disrupting the plant that pays for it. [Book a consultation](#consult) — ideally one that includes a floor walk.\n\n[Trust bar: plants served · uptime through deployments · reviews]\n\n## What We Build\n\n| System | The job it does | Plant-reality details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Production tracking / MES-class systems** | What's running, what's made, what's blocked — live | Operator-first data capture (scan > tap > type), downtime-reason coding, OEE that operators don't resent |\n| **Quality management (QMS)** | Inspections, non-conformances, CAPA, certs | In-line checkpoints, hold/release workflows, audit-ready traceability |\n| **Scheduling & planning tools** | The layer your ERP's module was too rigid for | Finite-capacity views, drag-and-drop resequencing, what-if without breaking the plan of record |\n| **Traceability & genealogy** | Lot/serial tracking from raw to shipped | Recall-readiness: full where-used in minutes, not a weekend of forensics |\n| **[Inventory & warehouse](/solutions/inventory-management-system/)** | Raw, WIP, and FG truth | Scanner-first [WMS flows](/solutions/warehouse-management-system/), cycle counting, staging for lines |\n| **Maintenance (CMMS-adjacent)** | PMs, work orders, asset history | Mobile-first for techs, downtime-linked prioritization, parts consumption |\n| **Shop-floor & field apps** | Work instructions, checklists, e-signoffs | Gloves-and-glare UX, kiosk mode, [offline-tolerant](/resources/software-engineering/offline-first-mobile/), zero-training targets |\n| **[Machine & IIoT integration](/services/iot-development/)** | Counts, states, and conditions off the equipment | OPC UA/Modbus/MQTT ingestion, edge buffering, PLC-vendor diplomacy |\n| **[Plant analytics](/solutions/bi-dashboards/)** | One version of the truth, by shift/line/SKU | Andon-style displays, scrap Pareto, [BI that answers \"why\"](/resources/cloud-devops/monitoring-observability-stack/) |\n| **[Manufacturing AI](/services/ai-application-development/)** | The proven use cases | [Vision QA](/resources/industry-insights/computer-vision-manufacturing-qa/), predictive-maintenance signals, document intelligence (COAs, specs, POs), scheduling-assist with human approval |\n\n## Integration: Making the ERP and the Floor Agree\n\nManufacturing builds are integration builds: **ERP** ([SAP](/platforms/sap/), [Dynamics](/platforms/microsoft-dynamics/), NetSuite, Epicor/Infor-class — orders down, confirmations and consumption up) · **machines** (OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT, and the serial-port archaeology older equipment demands) · **quality instruments** (scales, CMMs, testers) · **label/print systems** (ZPL and friends) · **EDI** for customer releases and ASNs ([we still speak it](/services/api-development/)) · **historians** where they exist. Two rules from hard experience: every machine feed gets edge buffering (networks blip; counts must not), and every ERP sync gets a [reconciliation report](/services/api-development/) — because \"the ERP says 400, the floor says 380\" is a meeting we end permanently.\n\n## Deployment Without Downtime\n\nPlants can't stop for software, so rollouts are engineered like changeovers: pilot on one line/cell with paper running parallel · cutover between shifts with rollback staged · training at the point of work in minutes-not-days doses · and [champions on the floor](/resources/digital-transformation/change-management-software-rollouts/) before go-live, because operator trust is the real deployment dependency. Systems that add operator effort get abandoned by Thursday; ours are designed to *remove* steps — scan-based capture that's faster than the clipboard it replaces.\n\n## Recent Manufacturing Work\n\n> **[PLACEHOLDER — 2–3 case studies: e.g., \"job shop production tracking: OEE visibility per line, expedites down X%\"; \"traceability system: recall drill from 3 days to 20 minutes\" — verifiable only]**\n\n## Cost Context\n\nShop-floor apps and quality modules: **$50K–$150K**. Production tracking/MES-class platforms: **$120K–$400K+** (machine-integration surface and line count are the drivers). ERP-integration layers: **$40K–$120K**. Every quote itemizes per-line and per-integration so a phased, one-line-first rollout is fundable. [Cost guides →](/resources/software-cost-guides/)\n\n## FAQ\n\n**Do we need a full MES, or something smaller?**\nMost mid-market plants need an MES-*class* system scoped to their actual pains — production visibility, quality capture, traceability — not a monolith with aerospace-grade configuration burden. We scope by pain ranked in the plant walk, and \"smaller than the acronym\" is our most common recommendation. [MES landscape →](/resources/industry-insights/smart-manufacturing-iiot-mes/)\n\n**Can you connect to our older machines?**\nAlmost always — modern PLCs speak OPC UA/Modbus; older equipment yields to sensors, signal taps, or (the honest fallback) operator-confirmed counts with scan validation. We'll survey the floor and tell you machine-by-machine what's connectable and what's not worth the cost.\n\n**Will this disrupt production during rollout?**\nNo — that's a design constraint, not a hope: pilot line first, parallel paper, between-shift cutovers, staged rollback. Our uptime-through-deployment record is the reference-call question to ask. [Deployment approach above.]\n\n**Our ERP vendor says their module does all this. Does it?**\nSometimes; usually the module models an idealized plant and yours is real. The honest test: can operators feed it truthfully in under 10 seconds per event? If not, you'll get compliance theater and stale data. We often build the capture-and-workflow layer *around* the ERP module rather than replacing it — [keep-the-core, fix-the-edge](/services/legacy-software-modernization/) is frequently the right architecture.\n\n**Where does AI genuinely pay in manufacturing now?**\nVision inspection on defined defect classes, predictive-maintenance signals where sensor history exists, document intelligence on specs/COAs/customer POs, and scheduling assistance with planner approval. We'll score your candidates honestly — including the \"not yet, your data isn't ready\" verdict when true. [AI development →](/services/ai-application-development/)\n\n**How long does a plant system take?**\nFirst line live: 3–4 months. Full multi-line rollout: 6–12 months, phased by line so value lands each quarter — and so the plant teaches us before we scale the design.\n\n---\n\n### [#consult] Book a Plant Software Consultation\nBring the spreadsheet with the version number in its filename — leave with a floor-walk plan, an architecture sketch, and a one-line-first quote.\n[Form] [Calendar embed]",
